<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aenona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nin is a small town on a lagoon islet in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>. As Aenona it was a Liburnian and then a Roman town, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a seat of the Croatian dukes and kings and, from the 9th century, of a bishopric whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Gregory of Nin is remembered as a champion of the Slavonic liturgy. The tiny <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathedral-of-the-holy-cross-aghtamar">Church of the Holy Cross</a> survives from that period.</p></div>
